\begin{abstract}
Prompt and configuration search picks the best of many candidates on benchmark items and reports that score.
We measure its gap to the chosen configuration's fresh accuracy on 1500 MMLU-Pro items and 128 configurations of Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct.
Searching 100 configurations on 30 items reports $0.390$ for a configuration of fresh accuracy $0.277$; the gap falls to $0.025$ at 400 items, and up to 100 items the reported gain over the default is at least four times the real one.
The search log predicts the gap of exhaustive search within $6\%$ up to 100 items, overshooting at most a third beyond.
With all configurations exactly equal, a naive test declares that tuning helped in $54\%$ of draws (nominal $5\%$).
At equal budget, a max-$t$ lower bound covers its nominal level on the real pool, is narrower than held-out and Bonferroni bounds, and spends no items; it undercovers over successive halving's evaluated configurations ($81$--$86\%$) and in exact-null synthetic worlds at 240 items ($91$--$93\%$).
A live DSPy search at 60 items has gap $0.066$, the replay $0.058$; we study one small model and one benchmark.
\end{abstract}
